Using the formula \( \text{distance} = \text{speed} \times \text{time} \), Aaron's distance is:
\[
\text{Aaron's distance} = \text{Aaron's speed} \times \text{Aaron's time}
\]
\[
\text{Aaron's distance} = (2v) \times 16 = 32v
\]
### Step 4: Relate Aaron's distance to Vanessa's distance
\[
\text{Vanessa's distance} = 2 \times \text{Aaron's distance}
\]
Substitute the values:
\[
4 = 2 \times (32v)
\]
Simplify:
